  "documents": [
    {
      "about": "The example of RFC 8785, 3.2.2 to 3.2.4: numbers, a string's escapes, literals, key order.",
      "json": "{\n  \"numbers\": [333333333.33333329, 1E30, 4.50, 2e-3, 0.000000000000000000000000001],\n  \"string\": \"\\u20ac$\\u000F\\u000aA'\\u0042\\u0022\\u005c\\\\\\\"\\/\",\n  \"literals\": [null, true, false]\n}",
      "canonical": "{\"literals\":[null,true,false],\"numbers\":[333333333.3333333,1e+30,4.5,0.002,1e-27],\"string\":\"€$\\u000f\\nA'B\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\"/\"}",
      "sha256": "2d5e01a318d0f0879ab568c4be289c8b1f64ef8921a53c6277d5e069978baacb"
    },
    {
      "about": "Keys are sorted by their UTF-16 code units (RFC 8785, 3.2.3), so U+1F600 (a surrogate pair, D83D DE00) comes before U+FB33.",
      "json": "{\"\\u20ac\": 1, \"\\r\": 2, \"\\ufb33\": 3, \"1\": 4, \"\\ud83d\\ude00\": 5, \"\\u0080\": 6, \"\\u00f6\": 7}",
      "canonical": "{\"\\r\":2,\"1\":4,\"\":6,\"ö\":7,\"€\":1,\"😀\":5,\"דּ\":3}",
      "sha256": "0d922ac8e15a6d17d5d50fab064983e86009ebae7b5998cbfc4ed172e6ff74a9"
    },
    {
      "about": "A number is written as its value, however the document wrote it: 6.0 and 6 are the same, -0 is 0.",
      "json": "[6.0, 6, -0.0, 0.035, 1e21, 1e-7, 123456789012345680000, -2.5e-6, 5291300.0, 612.40, 1.0E+2]",
      "canonical": "[6,6,0,0.035,1e+21,1e-7,123456789012345680000,-0.0000025,5291300,612.4,100]",
      "sha256": "0fcfeb9e339025d4a80f42006ad35966592640ccb73f0794cae3f5e970594879"
    },
    {
      "about": "Whitespace and key order of the document do not count; nested objects are sorted at every level; text is kept as it is, not normalised (ü as one code point and as u + U+0308 stay different).",
